Coltrane the Necromancer (named for John, not Robbie, in case you were
wondering) made it all the way yesterday. In keeping with the class, I
decided to play spellmaster as much as possible. (Of course you can't do
_only_ that, so having that +7 FB on the side really helped too! Best
weapon I've found outside Mjollnir and the ToM.) I really think spells
could be redesigned in NetHack. With a 21/23 Int/Wis the mana used was
pretty small, but the food consumption was very high. Coltrane went into
Gehennom with all the food he could carry (and you can collect plenty in
the castle) and made it back up with 2 food rations left.

The other constraint is that high level spells can only be used a very
few times before the spellbook fades, so a steady supply of magic
markers is necessary. Coltrane learnt two level-7 spells (cancellation,
finger of death) and was able to cast each exactly twice before fading
the book.

At high Int/Wis, I think all these constraints on spellcasting should
be reduced: the rates of food/magic consumption, and spellbook fading
and relearning, should all be made steep inverse functions of Int/Wis.
Then Weaponless the Wizard would be a real possibility.

Other notes:

There's a huge number of tengus on the quest: no wizard need worry about
having t&tc beyond this point.

This time I also decided to maximize loot, instead of just dashing down
and back. The usual procedure was: cast magic mapping in each maze to
locate the stairs, dig a short path from stair to stair (never genocide
Hs if you wan't to do this), then cast detect treasure, then ^T around
and collect loot. Even so, I managed only 4.6M points, I don't know how
people manage 7 and 8, and someone even claimed 18M on rgh once. There
was no Ludios on this trip (I've _never_ been there yet) though I
religiously visited each vault I found.

The paralysis trick is supposed not to work in 3.1.3. However, after I
tried it I never saw the bugger again. (I do vanilla 3.1.3 for unix.)

The clairvoyance spell is useful on no-map levels.

Favourite moment: finding Death and Famine lined up, I cast polymorph
(level 6) at them. Result (I kid you not: this really happened) was a
sewer rat and a homunculus! On the astral plane. Ho ho! I was already
grinning ear to ear at this point after having attempted to convert a
Ptah altar: the annoyed-god act is one of the best things in the game.
Henceforth, I don't go up there without polymorph. Seems to be _the_
way to deal with Horsemen: I don't see how they could resurrect after
this. (I verified that you can eat/offer the corpses of polymorphed
Horsemen.)

